course: dictionary
lesson: 5419
title: Lacy: delicate patterns
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Lacy describes something that looks like lace: fine and delicate, with a pattern of tiny holes or thin lines. Read the sentence for clues. «A lacy snowflake drifts down slowly» tells you the snowflake is light and delicate, not heavy.
explain: The clues also come from the other half of the sentence: «a hailstone is a solid ball of ice and pelts down». Solid and pelts show that a hailstone is the opposite of lacy: heavy and without holes.
explain: To check your meaning, swap it in. «A delicate, holey snowflake drifts down slowly» still makes sense, so lacy must mean delicate with a pattern of holes or thin lines.
fact: Lacy means made of lace, or looking like lace.
fact: Something lacy has a delicate pattern of holes or thin lines.
fact: «A lacy snowflake» drifts down slowly and looks delicate.
fact: Lacy is an adjective: lacy, lacier, laciest.
fact: Common phrases: lacy collar, lacy pattern, lacy frost.
exercise: «A lacy snowflake drifts down slowly.» What does lacy mean here? | *Delicate, with a pattern of tiny holes or thin lines | Solid and heavy | Coloured bright red | Loud and fast
exercise: Which word in «a hailstone is a solid ball of ice» shows that a hailstone is NOT lacy? | snowflake | *solid | drifts | pelts
exercise: Which of these is most likely to be lacy? | A bowling ball | *A lace collar with a pattern of holes | A tin of beans | A brick wall
test: Grandma's tablecloth has a lacy pattern. What does the pattern look like? | Loud and bright | Smooth and plain | *Full of fine holes and thin lines | Thick and solid
test: Which word is the comparison form of lacy? | lacy-est | lacely | *lacier | lacest
exam: Ella says the frost on the window is lacy. What does she mean? | It is hot and runny like soup | It is a solid sheet of ice with no shape at all | It makes a loud crashing noise | *It has a delicate pattern like lace
